.TH ETHTOOL 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
ethtool \- show or set a network card's Wake\-on\-LAN
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ethtool
.I interface
.br
.B ethtool \-s
.I interface
.RB [ wol
.BR s | d ]
.RB [ sopass
.IR message ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A network card can turn its computer on while the computer is off
(Wake\-on\-LAN): set a message, and a frame for the card (sent to it, or a
broadcast) that holds the message anywhere in its data switches the computer
on, as its power button would. The card keeps listening while the computer
is off, on the computer's power, and keeps the setting.
.PP
.B ethtool
.I interface
shows the setting:
.B Wake\-on: s
and the message
.RB ( "SecureOn password" ),
or
.B Wake\-on: d
(it doesn't wake).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "\-s " interface
Sets it, with:
.RS
.TP
.BI sopass " message"
The message that wakes it (and with it,
.BR "wol s" ).
.TP
.B wol s
Wakes with the message set before.
.TP
.B wol d
Doesn't wake.
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 for a bad argument or no such interface.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ethtool \-s wlan0 sopass letmein
ethtool wlan0
echo letmein | nc \-ub broadcast 9
.fi
The last, from another computer, turns this one on.
.SH NOTES
A real card wakes on a "magic packet" (its own MAC address, sixteen
times); SecureOn adds a password the packet must carry. Here the message
is all that's needed, as OpenComputers' wake message was, and it's found
anywhere in a frame's data, as a pattern\-matching card does. Port 9
(discard) is the usual one to send it to, though any will do.
.PP
Linux's
.B ethtool
sets much more of a card (its speed, offloads, rings);
.B wol
takes letters for the kinds of wake
.RB ( g
for a magic packet).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ifconfig (1),
.BR nc (1)
